BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 675.3 Td (trigger can be defined to be executed after an INSERT operation, and one before INSERT on the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 661.8 Td (same table. No triggers can be defined on views.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 618.6 Td (Before MySQL 8.0.28, inbuilt functions like UNIX_TIMESTAMP would return 0 after 03:14:07 UTC on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 605.1 Td (19 January 2038. In 2017, an attempt to solve the problem was submitted, but was not used for) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 591.6 Td (the final solution that was shipped in 2022.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12.5 Tf 56 545.0 Td (Deployment) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 494.4 Td (MySQL can be built and installed manually from source code, but it is more commonly installed) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 480.9 Td (from a binary package unless special customizations are required. On most Linux distributions,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 467.4 Td (the package management system can download and install MySQL with minimal effort, though) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 453.9 Td (further configuration is often required to adjust security and optimization settings.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 410.7 Td (Though MySQL began as a low-end alternative to more powerful proprietary databases, it has) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 397.2 Td (gradually evolved to support higher-scale needs as well. It is still most commonly used in) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 383.7 Td (small to medium scale single-server deployments, either as a component in a LAMP-based web) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 370.2 Td (application or as a standalone database server. Much of MySQL's appeal originates in its) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 356.7 Td (relative simplicity and ease of use, which is enabled by an ecosystem of open source tools such) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 343.2 Td (as phpMyAdmin. In the medium range, MySQL can be scaled by deploying it on more powerful) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 329.7 Td (hardware, such as a multi-processor server with gigabytes of memory.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 286.5 Td (There are, however, limits to how far performance can scale on a single server \('scaling up'\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 273.0 Td (so on larger scales, multi-server MySQL \('scaling out'\) deployments are required to provide) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 259.5 Td (improved performance and reliability. A typical high-end configuration can include a powerful) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 246.0 Td (source database \(formerly known as master database\) which handles data write operations and is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 232.5 Td (replicated to multiple replicas \(formerly known as slaves\) that handle all read operations. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 219.0 Td (source server continually pushes binlog events to connected replicas so in the event of failure) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 205.5 Td (a replica can be promoted to become the new source, minimizing downtime. Further improvements) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 192.0 Td (in performance can be achieved by caching the results from database queries in memory using) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 178.5 Td (memcached, or breaking down a database into smaller chunks called shards which can be spread) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 165.0 Td (across a number of distributed server clusters.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12.5 Tf 56 118.4 Td (High availability software) Tj ET
