BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 772.5 Td (WebScaleSQL was a software branch of MySQL 5.6, and was announced on 27 March 2014 by Facebook,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 759.0 Td (Google, LinkedIn and Twitter as a joint effort to provide a centralized development structure) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 745.5 Td (for extending MySQL with new features specific to its large-scale deployments, such as building) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 732.0 Td (large replicated databases running on server farms. Thus, WebScaleSQL opened a path toward) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 718.5 Td (deduplicating the efforts each company had been putting into maintaining its own branch of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 705.0 Td (MySQL, and toward bringing together more developers. By combining the efforts of these) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 691.5 Td (companies and incorporating various changes and new features into MySQL, WebScaleSQL aimed at) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 678.0 Td (supporting the deployment of MySQL in large-scale environments. The project's source code is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 664.5 Td (licensed under version 2 of the GNU General Public License, and is hosted on GitHub.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12.5 Tf 56 617.9 Td (OurDelta) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 567.3 Td (The OurDelta distribution, created by the Australian company Open Query \(later acquired by) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 553.8 Td (Catalyst IT Australia\), had two versions: 5.0, which was based on MySQL, and 5.1, which was) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 540.3 Td (based on MariaDB. It included patches developed by Open Query and by other notable members of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 526.8 Td (the MySQL community including Jeremy Cole and Google. Once the patches were incorporated into) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 513.3 Td (the MariaDB mainline, OurDelta's objectives were achieved and OurDelta passed on its build and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 499.8 Td (packaging toolchain to Monty Program \(now MariaDB Plc\).) Tj ET
